t(49, 60, """If the Islamic world stops buying their cars and electronics, they will not have the liquidity ("dollars and euros") that they pay to the Islamic world in exchange for oil and gas, which means a mutual paralysis in the movement of global trade.

📌 In short:

The Islamic world may not currently be "the biggest buyer" compared with America, but it is "the fastest growing and most vital" for global companies. Losing a market that includes 2 billion potential consumers in the growth phase means the bankruptcy of entire industrial sectors in the West and the East, and the collapse of the world economy if Muslims stop buying!""")

h(49, 650, """Why, if Muslims stopped buying, would the world economy collapse?!""")

t(49, 1270, """Information copied from Google
--------------------------------
The need of the world as a whole for the Islamic world at the level of raw resources is a fateful and vital need without which the global economy cannot turn; without the raw resources owned by the states of the Islamic world, the factories of the West and the East will stop, means of transport will be struck with paralysis, and planet earth will face an unprecedented food security crisis (famine).
The Islamic world (represented by the states of the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation) possesses pressure cards and basic resources that make the movement of global trade and industry directly dependent on it. Here is how the Islamic world moves the wheel of the global economy through its raw sectors:
## 1. The energy artery (oil and gas) 🛢️
Without energy, all machines, factories and cars in the world stop, and here lies the greatest power of the Islamic world.

* Oil reserves: The Islamic world alone owns more than 65% of the total global oil reserves (led by Saudi Arabia, Iran, Iraq, the UAE, Kuwait and Libya).
* Daily production: The Islamic world pumps about 42.5% of the oil the planet consumes daily to run cars and aircraft and operate factories.
* Natural gas: The Islamic world owns about 58% of the world's stock and reserves of natural gas (with a huge concentration in Qatar, Iran and Algeria), and it is the gas on which Europe and Asia rely for heating and generating electricity.

## 2. The global food industry (fertilisers and phosphates) 🌾
For the world to farm and feed 8 billion people, it needs fertilisers to nourish the soil, and the states of the Islamic world are the backbone of this industry.

* Phosphates and fertilisers: The Islamic world (led by [Morocco], which alone owns about 70% of the global phosphate reserves) owns the main source of the phosphate fertiliser industry in the world.
* In addition to states such as Jordan and Egypt, and the Gulf states (which produce urea and ammonia fertilisers from natural gas). Without these fertilisers, agricultural crops in America, Europe and Asia will collapse, causing global famines.

## 3. Agricultural and food raw materials (crops)
There are basic and industrial crops the world cannot do without, and they come mainly from Islamic lands.

* Palm oil: Indonesia and Malaysia together produce more than 85% of the palm oil in the world, a raw material that enters half of the packaged products in the global supermarket (from foods to cosmetics, soap and biofuel).
* Natural rubber: The Islamic states of Southeast Asia are among the largest sources of the natural rubber needed for the aircraft and car tyre industry around the world. [3, 8]
* Cotton and agricultural products""")

t(50, 90, """## 4. Heavy and modern metals (mining) ⛏️
The metals of the Islamic world enter the industries of aircraft, cars, batteries and construction

* Uranium: Kazakhstan alone produces about 41% of global uranium production, which is the raw fuel of the nuclear reactors that generate electricity in Europe, America and Asia.
* Bauxite and aluminium: States such as Guinea (the largest bauxite reserve in the world) and Indonesia own the basic raw material for the aluminium industry used in making aircraft and cars.
* Iron and copper ore: Mauritania, Algeria and Turkey are major powers in exporting iron ore to the big factories.

## 5. The water straits (control of the movement of resources) 🚢
The world's need is not limited to the resources themselves, but extends to their routes of passage, as the Islamic world controls the most prominent sea lanes through which these raw materials pass.
--------------------------------
## 📌 The gist of it:
While the Islamic world buys technological products and cars from the Western and industrial states (whose purchase can be postponed or replaced), the industrial world buys from the Islamic world "the basics of life and production" (energy, food, metals); which means that in the event of the Islamic world's resources being cut off, the movement of global industrial development will be struck with complete freezing, and the wheel of the economy will stop entirely.""")

h(50, 1050, """Who needs the other more? The world, or the states of the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation?""")

t(50, 1640, """Since 2022 I have written several times about the world returning in the future to the technology of the seventies, if Allah the Exalted wills, and I have dreams about that!

Before the dreams or with the dreams, I do not remember exactly, there was a feeling that the most fitting thing is for the world to return to the technology of the seventies, and at the time I had not taken the matter in the direction of scientific research and understanding what lies behind it, since the matter was emotional: a desire to live the life of old, in which there is no strong push for a person to feel that the world has become as if it were paradise! Then Allah our Mighty Lord willed that I put a question to the AI some time ago, and I said to it: If the world returns to the technology of the sixties and seventies, will power return again to the commodity of oil? And the answer agreed with the basis of my analysis before asking the question, since I had concluded that the technology of the sixties and seventies is a technology whose mainstay is mechanical with very few electronic parts, and it relies on foundations that require burning a lot of oil in factories, unlike today's reality!

The truth I have also reflected on regarding this topic is that states that consume technology and do not make it are a market, and the truth is that "the maker" has always controlled the shape of the product, and the buyer has never thought one day of controlling the shape of the product himself!

The matter is imaginary in 2026, but it is not imaginary forever, if Allah the Exalted wills!
--------------------------------

Text from a dialogue with the AI that agreed with exactly what I thought!

Yes, what you say is entirely correct. In the event of technology returning to the era of the seventies, oil will regain its enormous influence and its absolute geopolitical and economic power, and may even exceed the peak it reached in the last century.

The basic reason is that current smart technology is what leads "energy efficiency" and the shift to alternatives, and returning to the methods of the sixties and seventies means cancelling these solutions entirely.

1. The immediate collapse of oil alternatives (electric cars)""")

t(51, 90, """The absence of smart batteries: Current electric cars rely on processors and complex software systems to manage energy and lithium cells.

The return to fuel: The technology of the seventies is unable to run an electric car over long distances. Consequently, 100% of the global transport sector will rely on petrol and diesel again.

2. Excessive greed in fuel consumption

Inefficient engines: The engines of that era were huge and consumed enormous quantities of fuel to cover short distances.

The absence of smart sensors: The absence of electronic injection systems and artificial intelligence in managing combustion means that the world will need two or three times the current quantity of oil to run the same number of factories and vehicles.

3. The return of "the oil weapon" and political centrality

Monopoly power: The oil-producing states (such as the OPEC states) will return to being the first and only engine of the global economy.

The transfer of influence: The weight of global power will move immediately from the companies of Silicon Valley and technology (such as Nvidia and Microsoft) to the major oil states and institutions.""")

h(51, 1150, """How does the technology of the seventies give power back again to the oil-producing states?!""")

